A||||26||filthyscarecrow||brake question about GA upgrade||||1||
Z||000000||filthyscarecrow||11-18-2001||02:43 AM||||i know ogre is not fond of this one, but i'm lookin at putting GA front brakes on my 86. i'm putting a turbo on too, so i might as wel be able to stop it, right?<BR>okay, for the swap, the caliper mounting bracket has to be ground down to allow the calipers to be offset to the rotors correctly, right? can you just have whomever is turnign down the old discs into hubs grind 1/4" off the face of the old disc instead of grinding the caliper mounting bracket? this would just move the rotor in instead of movign the caliper out and preserve the front track. i saw a pic of a rotor/hub and it looked like it had plenty of meat on it to do this. anyone have any suggestions? i'd also like to cut some gas slots in them before i put them on. anyone tried this? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||11-18-2001||07:12 AM||||Chris Moore and Lisa did those to hers last month on all 4 wheels, might ask her what they did.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||11-18-2001||07:21 AM||||I did all 4 corners on the Grape Ape (2 or 3 Fieros ago).  I did it right as I swapped to a Blazer M/C and SS brake lines.  The results were very disappointing to say the least.  I could tell a difference, but probably could have seen the same improvement just by using new, OEM parts (just about everything was shot).  Do what you wish, but I think you'll be wasting your time and money.||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||11-18-2001||11:28 AM||||This is the info I used when I did it. I was more than happy with the end result.<BR> <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/index.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||filthyscarecrow||11-18-2001||05:12 PM||||i wasn't planning on swapping out the MC. ogre says in the cave that swapping the lines front to rear on the proportioning block should give similar brake bias to stock and allow the fast take up feature that normally operates the rear brakes to fill the front pistons faster without more pedal travel (anyone tried this?). i hope i understood that right, but that seems like a better and cheaper way to go. <p>[This message has been edited by filthyscarecrow (edited 11-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||jelly2m8||11-18-2001||06:49 PM||||The purpose of ginding material off the caliper mount, is because the GA rotors are thicker, and will interfere with the caliper mount. Machining material off the face of the hub will only add to this interference.<P> The GA brake upgrade does not make a huge difference in outright braking power. <P> What it does do is give you better modulation of the brakes, better fade reisitance, cheaper replacement parts.<P> The GA rotors eliminate the shaking of the stock rotors during high speed stops, as they can dissipate the heat much more efficiantly.<P> The only downfall I have with the GA conversion at 4 corners is the loss of the E-brake.<P> I cannot afford the more expensive big brake upgrades, and therefore am very happy with the GA conversion. All my pre-88 Fieros will have the GA brakes shortely.||||reg||1
Z||000006||filthyscarecrow||11-18-2001||07:27 PM||||yeah, i figured the grinding part out when i read the link in PBJ's post. good stuff, hopefully i can get on a lathe sometime late at night and get teh old rotors turned down. i'm not lookin for decresased pedal effort, just better fade control. that's why y was gonna try to cut some shalow gas slots in them too, hopefully help keep pads from glazing, and help eliminate the superhot gas that builds up between the pad and rotor under heavy braking. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||jelly2m8||11-18-2001||07:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by filthyscarecrow:<BR><B> i'm not lookin for decresased pedal effort, just better fade control. that's why y was gonna try to cut some shalow gas slots in them too, hopefully help keep pads from glazing, and help eliminate the superhot gas that builds up between the pad and rotor under heavy braking. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> I think you will be satisfied with the GA brakes then, best of luck.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierohoho||11-18-2001||09:45 PM||||Carl, I could put you in touch with someone who turns the stock front rotors down for the upgrade, not sure how much it costs though.<P>I was just at his house Sunday and he had several sets laying around, they may be taken but I could ask if you are interested.<P>My friend did the conversion to his car and left the master cylinder stock and said it was fine as is, no line swaping at the metering block and good brakes.<P>My friend grinds the old rotor hub down so it's .010 (? 10,000th) undersize so it's a tight fit inside the GA rotor.<P>PM me if you're interested, HoHo.<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by fierohoho (edited 11-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||11-19-2001||09:13 PM||||The Front Only GA upgrade has issues. I'm not a huge fan of it in it's present form.<P>I'm not going to rehash the whole cave article, but I will try to explain a couple things better.<P>FSCrow, someone around here was going to try the line swap on the MC but never posted if they did or what the results were. They were having issues with MC volume on a front only conversion. (If they did post, I haven't seen it...)<P>Here's the reason why I was looking at changing the MC plumbing....... It should work but I have no way to test it short of doing the front only upgrade to my own car. I'm not going to do that untill, at the very least, I find a better answer to rotor centering. (Plus my OE front rotors are good. I'm not cutting them for a test...)<P>If you have problems with MC volume, then try swapping the MC outlets and TELL US what the results are.<P>The purpose for swapping the 2 MC outlets is two fold.<P>One, It puts the calipers needing the most volume on the MC outlet with the most volume.<P>Two, Because of reason one it should solve any imediate fluid shortage but just as importantly it will increase reserve capacity. (Reserve capacity is the amount of piston travel available between normal MC operation and a dry MC.)<P>The object is to setup the MC to Never have either circuit squeazed dry or even close to it. You want to keep the brakes working even if the calipers have a clearance problem. (Specifically, Too much pad clearance with rotor) <P>If the rears have a clearance problem, and they usually do, then you have crappy braking.... but if the front brakes ever experiance the same problem because of a bad caliper, warped rotor, or wheel bearing getting sloppy, then you could have very little or no brakes at all.<P>remember the GA caliper needs considerably more fluid to cover the same distance as OE calipers. You will all but eliminate the pedal reserve for that circuit with the OE MC setup.<P>On the other hand... the rear claipers need allot less fluid to close. The secondary outlet should be able to handle them even if they are out of adjustment. They'd have to be pretty far off just to use the same fluid as the OE fronts.<P>Keep in mind.... Both MC outlets develope the same working pressure. Swapping ports <u>will not</u> change brake proportioning! It will only affect available fluid volume.<P>In Fiero, proportioning is determined almost entirely by the differance in front/rear piston area and the prop valve in the balance block.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||filthyscarecrow||11-19-2001||10:39 PM||||hey, somebody's got to try it, right? (dipping my foot in the water...)||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||11-20-2001||04:00 AM||||It... frieghtens me... how cheap and cavilear some people are with their own lives... <P>Come on guys, a coffin costs way more than a set of real brakes. (Archie-style 10.25", or Held Motorsports full-on 12 or 13" setup.)<P>The stock setup, while not great, when functioning properly, WILL lock the wheels...<BR>It just takes too long to build up boost.  <BR>Our brakes have turbo lag.  Erals/FieroStore SS lines help a bit.  Either Held Motorsports kit is like running into a wall...<P>Please guys, your lives, and possibly mine, ride on your brakes.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000012||theogre||11-20-2001||07:15 PM||||FSCrow, Do Not alter brake rotors yourself. That’s a good way to have a rotor explode during heavy braking. Drilling/slotting has to be done in very specific ways and varies depending on the specific rotor on hand. Also, it’s just not needed with modern friction materials. Willwood and Baer offer that mainly to satisfy customer’s weight and appearance desires. It’s covered on both their web sites.<P>Also, you can’t cut the rotor to clear the caliper bracket. Even if it looks like there’s enough material, you’d leave the rotor below minimum thickness.<P>Jelly, Correct, cutting the hub face would make the bracket problem worse <u>with a GA rotor.</u> Cutting the hub would be used in conjunction with another rotor. What other rotor can fit the bill here I don’t know. <P>Ben, I've been saying that for a couple years now. I’m not being cheap or cavalier about it. My whole cave article, the brake service and fluid articles that followed it are the result of the GA upgrades “sounding too good to be true.” Once I figured out what I didn’t like about the GA set, I went on with the OE system to figure out why everyone thought it was so bad. I’ve applied nearly all the service and fluid info to my own car. My brakes were in ok shape to start with but now they are much better. (And they are still bone stock.)<P>In very short, I agree with you 100% that Fiero brakes are not inherently bad. When you compare them to other cars on the road, they actually compare very well. They simply aren’t made to accept the abuse and neglect that commonly befalls them. The number one problem with Fiero, and most other, brake systems is poor service. (And to a point, a design issue in the plug faced rear piston, but that’s another topic. It’s covered in the cave… That issue is the cause of one recall.)<P>I don’t agree about that lag thing. If OE brakes feel laggy then something is wrong. Most likely the soft lines or the MC. Soft lines could be internally swollen or MC could have a flaky QT valve. One of the most common killers of soft lines is spraying them with penetrating oil when trying to remove the bleeder or banjo bolt. The oil soaks thru the jacket and swells the guts. (BTW, I’m working on a better explanation of how the Quick Take-up MC works...) ||||reg||1
Z||000013||MrFieroUK||11-20-2001||07:56 PM||||I've fitted GA brakes to the front and the larger Blazer MC (kit purchased from the Fiero Store). I tried to swap lines this weekend past BUT... GM master cylinders use two different sized lines from the MC to the balance block - each line has its OWN flare nut thread size - and, the dimple in the MC & balance block which seals against the (different sized) pipe flares is also different. So you can't JUST swap the lines over. Two possibilities - i've yet to work out which way to go;<P>FIRST swap out the balance block for another GM type from a rear wheel drive application (Blazer?), where, I assume the "normal" rules apply and the larger line (from the secondary piston in the MC) feeds the rear brake circuit in the balance block - it may be neccessary to swap out the proportioning valve components to keep the Fiero's factory spec. in place.<P>SECOND use adapter flare nuts which reduce the larger outlets/inlets to the smaller pipe fitting. There APPEARS to be one on the front brake outlet from the balance block?!? If this is possible, then two would be required - one for the MC secondary outlet, and one for the primary (front) balance block inlet. You would also need two smaller size flare nuts. GM seems to do this on all its vehicles (including NEW cars equiped with ABS), presumably to prevent incorrect coupling of the MC to the rest of the circuit(s) - interesting that it doesn't use the same procedure to exit the balance block/proportioning valve.<P>*NOTE* My braking system was completely overhauled with fresh STOCK everything including rebuilt calipers, pads, fluid etc. about 500 miles before the GA front upgrade.<P>I was disappointed with the GA front brake conversion to start with. The pedal felt way too spongy. HOWEVER, when I examined the amount of take-up required by the GA calipers before the pads contacted the rotors, it was apparent there was no real slop in the system. There appears to be A LOT MORE take up requirement in the GA calipers than in the stock Fiero pieces.<P>Also, GA pads are semi-metallic from most brands and need a fair bit of heat to get them biting.<P>Experiment: try driving along a private or empty stretch of road (for safety), warm up the brakes with a few hard stops, then as you apply the brakes again, mentally IGNORE the initial low pressure period of travel (as the pads move through the take-up distance), THEN note the brake feel as the pads start to bite. It is quite apparent there is significant bite in the new front system. the front brakes will lock-up with a LOT LESS effort than stock!<P>The subjective "feel" of the Fiero's brakes will be determined by HOW QUICKLY the pads begin to bite. A substantial movement in the pedal before retardation is felt will lead one to suspect "spongy-ness". Therefore, Ogre is probably correct in suggesting an MC line swap, where the quick take up on the primary circuit would act on the front brakes, not rear. <P>Why did GM offer this strange set-up on the Fiero?? I can only assume that it is because the majority of weight is over the rear wheels and there would be less mass (weight in motion) transfer to the front brakes, under hard braking, than you would get with a front engined vehicle - possibly leading to premature front wheel lock-up. However, the rear brakes in a stock set-up generate VERY LITTLE braking force compared with the fronts. For example; 200 or so miles after the stock system was refurbished, the car was due its annual inspection. In the UK that means a brake roller test where the braking force in Kgs is measured. The fronts managed a platry 350 to 390 Kg each side at lock-up, but the rears only achieved a pathetic 100Kg per side and didn't reach lock -up! (This was the same figure as the E-brake made.)<P>Something isn't quite right here! I've just driven a 2000 Mustang V6 with similar sized rotors and pads (visual assesment) to the Fiero, vented front, solid rear - its brakes were worlds better - the Mustang is about 400Lbs heavier...<P>After resolving the line swap, I will consider trying a set of carbonmetalic pads to see if the cold stopping bite is improved - Anyone have any feedback on carbonmetalic pads? Anyone know of a source of non metallic pads? I don't mind the increased wear rate - I DO MIND poor cold performance and rotors consumed rapidly!<P>Other issues;<BR>On a stock suspension, the brake hose banjo bolt on the GA calipers will foul on the shock strut lower mount when the suspension is at full extension. My Fiero's front wheels STILL have problems with tracking straight under heavy (near lock-up) braking - even though all the suspension bushes have been checked for excessive play etc. This could be a legacy of the adoption of archaic T-car suspension layout.<P>The points above could be a good excuse for a coil over conversion - anyone tried this?||||reg||1
Z||000014||filthyscarecrow||11-20-2001||09:48 PM||||sorry to be pissy here, but i am not an idiot. i don't test things where there are other people around, and i don't flog new parts right away. as for slotting the rotors, they would be very shallow slots and not much, if any, thinner than factory specs. we MAKE our own brakes for our race cars. (slotted, no less...) <P>i'll let you know how it goes.||||reg||1
Z||000015||MrFieroUK||11-21-2001||07:25 AM||||There was no intention to be condescending. I don't know YOUR technical background, wether your inbusiness or just a hands-on enthusiast, and I assume others of a non-technical background reading this thread, would appreciate all the info. available. I wasn't preaching, meerely reporting all the facts to hand... Sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>Yes I agree don't test in traffic - do it on a deserted road, abandoned airfield runway etc. - I should have been clearer.||||reg||1
Z||000016||lowCG||11-21-2001||08:59 AM||||Do let us(me)know when you find a good source of wheel studs;the closest I could find still aren't correct in that they spin in the hub when torqued,and are real fun to take apart after that.<BR>My car does stop like it should have from the factory,but putting the BMW/Subaru parts on was a real hassle I admit,and proably wouldn't do it again.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Eric_PA||11-21-2001||09:43 AM||||I did the conversion on the front and I don't like it. Stops a bit better, but I put on new SS braided brake lines new rear calipers and rotors along with all new pads. I was very dissapointed. A year later I constantly hear noise coming from the front rotors shifting when on the brakes.<P>||||reg||13
Z||000018||theogre||11-21-2001||12:17 PM||||FSCrow,,, I don’t know what you do away from here…. Most people asking about drill/slot don’t have the tools or anything else to do it right. I don’t think you’ll gain anything doing that to the GA rotors. Try them before you cut them.<P>MrFieroUK,<P>I’m having a little trouble following what you wrote; I must need more caffeine... <P>A spongy/low pedal at any time is not normal. Sounds like there is still an air bubble someplace. Even “race” pads shouldn’t cause low pedal cold, they simply don’t grab well. Pedal feel/height should be constant. Before you think about changing the MC plumbing, try using a vacuum or power bleeder on it. Pedal bleeding often has trouble moving enough fluid to get all the air, especially if its up by the MC. Hopefully the bubble isn’t in the MC. If it is you may have to take it off and bench bleed it again.<P>The other common issue in Fiero with low pedal and/or poor OE rear performance is rear pad clearance. Even new/rebuilt rear calipers don’t always want to self adjust right after a brake job. That’s why GM put out the TSB I developed into the manual adjustment procedure in my cave.<P>If it still seems like a pad problem... what pads exactly are on it? I’ve never seen poor cold performance with regular retail pads, even semi metallic. It’s nearly always “performance” or “race” pads that do that. I’d try dropping some regular Raybestos or other normal retail pads into it and see if that solves it. (All I run are semi-met retail pads... I’ve never had cold braking problems or high rotor wear rates. GA and Fiero are both specified as using semi-met pads.)<P>Far as I knew, the full size blazer MC is a straight bore job. If that were the case, swapping the lines on the blazer MC would not help you... Even if it’s a QT MC, the QT function may be of limited value. The QT bore is 36mm in the one’s I’ve seen. That’s not very much larger than the 28mm main bore but it may still be enough to help.<P>I’m talking about retaining the OE MC for the front only GA conversion. Putting the Blazer MC in a car with OE rear brakes pretty much kills the rear brakes. (Bigger MC piston means less line pressure. Less line pressure, less brakes.) If you thought the rears were weak in the OE setup, they are worse with the Blazer MC. As originally configured, the OE MC is tight on fluid volume for the GA calipers. The line swap should fix that.<P>While it probably seems that way, I never meant you just swap the lines.... you have to make new ones. Bending used lines very often breaks them or causes them to break shortly there after. You also save the old lines in case you need them. Here, you can get spare nuts from the salvage yards. I imagine you can but you may have to dig around more.<P>You are just about have to swap the lines between the MC and balance block. Adaptors may be needed to do it. (I never said it would be easy...)<P>Should you try replacing the balance block, you can’t use a balance block from a front disk/rear drum vehicle… Those usually have a “hold off” function in them that would cause problems in a 4wheel disk set. The hold off function intentionally delays front brake application to allow the rear drums to close their slack space. <P>The thing that looks like an adaptor in the end of the Fiero balance block is not an adaptor. It’s the “plug” so to speak that the closes the bore hole for the balance bar. GM hooked up the front lines like that simply to avoid adding another T fitting to the car. You can find a cutaway view of the valve in the archives at <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> <P>Different size lines/nuts is likely an assembly line aid more than anything else. When you’ve got seconds to decide and assemble the parts, a choice like “big line in front” is simpler than figuring out which one is bent how. (The booster MC and balance block are dropped into the car as an assembled unit. I believe that’s how GM actually buys them.)<P>What you are describing about the calipers likely means the GA unit is a “low drag” design. Those calipers are made so the seals will pull the pistons back more. It’s done to increase fuel economy.<P>We use brake dyno’s here in Delaware as part of inspection so I’m familiar with what you are saying... I wish I could get access to one of them so I could test the different brake configurations. Then we could put an end to nearly all the guesswork very quickly. <P>If your rear calipers were working right, what you saw in your brake dyno results may also be related to the Chevy Corvair. GM intentionally reduced the rear brake line pressure in Fiero to prevent rear wheel lockup and spinouts that were common in the rear engine Corvair a couple decades previous. (Read “Unsafe at Any Speed” by Ralf Nader.)<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000019||filthyscarecrow||11-21-2001||12:26 PM||||i was also going to cut an adapter on the lathe to make the rotors hubcentric- to make the round part of the hub fit the round hole in the rotor to keep them in place. nice tight fit. <BR>UK, i wasn't being pissy towards you, it was mostly artherd. i guess there are people on this forum who might not think it a bad idea to test new setups on the busy streets, but i'm not one of them. like i said, we make the brake rotors from scratch for our formula car, and that thing has the most incredible brakes i've ever driven. and yes, they're slotted. <BR>people don't seem to understand slotting rotors. it doesn't cool them down. it's not supposed to. under heavy braking, a thin layer of superhot gas builds up between the pads and the rotors, and the pads effectively "hydroplane" (i don't know the gaseous term) on this layer of gas, and have less contact with the rotors. the slots give that gas somewhere to go adn they also scrape the glaze off the pads, reducing pad fade. i have a set on my integra as well, and the difference is night and day, even over the integra's already kickass brakes. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||MrFieroUK||11-21-2001||04:01 PM||||Sorry Ogre,<P>I posted that reply at 01:30 GMT and I guess "my" use of english at that time of the morning wasn't very concise!<P>When I say spongy - I mean dead travel on the pedal. The brake hydraulics are fine, they've had 1.5 litres of DOT4 vacuum bled through them (I use a shop air line driven system ~ very effective, even on ABS equiped vehicles.) That dead travel equates to the caliper take up distance before the pads contact the rotor - i've visually checked this two ways with an assistant. (At first I was worried the new MC left some slack between the piston face and servo push rod, as I said the take up distance on the GA calipers is noticeably greater than the Fiero originals.<P>I don't like the dead travel - probably because I'm so used to vehicles which have a lot of inital bite in the first part of pedal travel.<P>The Blazer MC is a QT type. I remember mentally noting the larger diameter on the piston face (than the Fiero) when I swapped the two over - I also remember wondering whether the Blazer MC boss was going to fit inside the servo housing when I first compared the two MCs side by side! If you want figures , I'll have to take the MC out next time I'm working on the car.<P>I also take your point on rear caliper adjustment/rotor run-out etc. However, I took extra note of the pull off distance after the rebuild. Brakes are THE most important safety feature on your car, (other than the loose nut behind the wheel!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) So over the years I've always checked, double checked and then checked again to make sure everything is functioning to spec. or as well as can be expected. After tightening the pads to the rotors using the e-brake actuator screw, the rotors are spinning true and the pad back off is no more than (guessing from memory) about 0.5 millimeters ~ 20 thou. each side of the disc surface<P>You seem to agree with me that the reason GM backed off the rear brake pressure so much is down to the potential for tail heavy spin outs from rear brake lock up?<P>Now we have the situation where we need a greater volume of fluid to actuate the GA calipers which have a larger piston diameter and an agressive pull off.<P>On reflection, with the original set-up, the rear calipers WOULD need the QT function because they had the more aggressive pull off ~ especially with the effect of the E-brake mechanism, (which design, in my 'umble opinion, leaves a lot to be desired - Ford does a better disc e-brake.)<P>With front GA calipers the requirements are reversed. I'm therefore concerned about having sufficient pedal travel and fluid for this bigger volume requirement.<P>I hear what you say about GA master cylinder bore size and reduced pressure with larger bore size. It seems there must be scope for adjusting the pressure to the rear calipers to compensate and have the best of both (all?) worlds.<P>I've read the archive article on webelectric mags. site - very informative and yes larger diameter rotors will increase the effective braking torque. But, having driven heavier cars with similar brake set-ups to the Fiero with GA rotors, I am yet to be convinced there isn't a lot more can be done with the 9.75" diameter set up. The Fiero is not a heavy car when compared to other small 2 door Detroit iron such as the V6 Mustang, Chrysler Sebring etc.<P>I don't know what outside ambient temperatures are typical in your neck of the woods Ogre, but over here in Blighty we are now down around 32 to 45*F in the fall. That is enough to produce a noticeable difference in cold braking performanceduring the first 10 or so minutes of driving. Especially if you have to make an emergency stop as you pull out of your street, because of some errant child or pet. My fiero is not unusual in this phenomenon. My wife's ABS equiped 1999 Chrysler Neon (UK spec.) has the same problem particularly since it received new rotors and pads (at 23,000 miles) dealer installed, cold braking is a pig - warm them up and hey presto, good bite. On one occassion I travelled no more than 200 yards from a cold start before having to brake heavily for a stray cat dashing out in front of me. Even from 30 mph with a full on straight leg pedal push, with four adults in the car the Neon failed to lock its wheels or actuate the ABS - semi metalic brake pads installed by the dealer to "bed the new rotors in"! - The cat survived -  I am ignorant as to the different compounds used in brake friction material and wonder how much pads differ across brands to the "same" specification. I'm also too tired right now to dlve into my CFR49 to examine FMVSS105/135 to see if they shed light on material(s).<P>The concept of slotted/cross drilled rotors to remove hot surface gasses must have some merit - even for road cars? Would this not assist in reducing fade due to heavy braking? I've certainly experienced this during my road tests of the GA rotors, particularly when bedding them in - this included 3 full on emergency stops inducing limited front wheel lock up(the brakes smoked for 15 minutes after I parked up!)<P>The only GA brake pads I've located are semi-metallic - you mentioned Raybestos as a potential source - do you know which major chains stock them? I'm at a disadvantage here, working more than 3,000 miles from the closest part of the USA!<P>Your help and comments are much appreciated. Again, sorry for my confusing use of language (could this be a cultural thing? You know, hood vs bonnet fender vs wing etc.)<P>None of this is going to be diffinitive until the changes are made and roller brake tests are done. I intend to continue experimenting and will test my "seat-o-the-pants" impressions with figures obtained from a local friendly vehicle test station. More anon.<P>As ever, we experiment with these car's brakes at our own risks and anyone reading this should be aware that these are personal opinions from my own (limited) experience - with all due respect to any brake design engineers looking in. Cheers!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||11-21-2001||05:16 PM||||The OE rear brakes have fairly agressive pull off. Even the GM TSB has you adjusting them to 10-15 thousandths (inch) of total clearance.<P>Keep in mind tho that the rear piston is smaller than the OE front. So if we say the rears run at twice the clearance of the OE fronts, they don't use twice the fluid to do close... If the OE MC's secondary can run the big fronts, and some say it can manage the front only GA setup, then it should handle the OE rears. Again, I don't have a way to test it or all the figures to calc it. It's something to carefully try that might solve some issues.<P>The spread sheet in my cave has nearly all the info to work out the volume requirements. I don't know what the OE front caliper total clearance is. It's easy to measure... force the caliper and outer pad against the rotor then try feelers in the space between the inner pad and rotor. I'm guessing around 5-8 thousandths in a good caliper on a stable rotor. shouldn't be much more than that for a standard caliper.<P>Also note... The QT primary section also accelerates caliper closing in the secondary circuit. It just won't add fluid volume to the secondary. It's hard to explain.<P>Yeah, GM went chicken on the rear brakes. It's only in the prop valve as far as I can tell. The rear calipers should deliver more than enough brake for even the big motored Fieros out there.<P>There can be considerable differance between pad compounds. Not only between brands but even within differant product lines in the same brand. Unfortunatly the FMVSS probably won't help much. Standard 135 only sets up required performance and equipement for brake systems. (Like that annoying thing called a hand brake.) I haven't read all the others yet.<P>I'm not sure how much it will help, but try looking up the pads you need thru PartsAmerica.com, Autozone.com, napaonline.com, etc. Autozone and partsamerica list most things by the makers part number. You can also try raybestos.com, bendix.com, and <A HREF="http://www.federalmogul.com" TARGET=_blank>www.federalmogul.com</A>  (They own Ferodo and Wagner brands.) One or more of them may also be able to help via email or something.... If you have any family friends here maybe they can mail you a set. (I'm guessing you tried web sites of local/regional parts chains?)<P>I get pretty cold weather here. Down to 0F anyway. Used to get colder in upstate NY. Makes me wonder where your brake service people are getting pads and what they are using... The problems you describe would never pass FMVSS tests here. NHTSA likely would recall them for that kind of thing. (NHTSA does regulate allot of aftermarket stuff. There's a big thing right now over aftermarket lighting products.) 571.135 is really particular that brakes behave the almost the same from cold until very hot. like just about over heated hot. there's some allowance for cold but being nearly unable to stop is well outside limits.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||MrFieroUK||11-21-2001||06:31 PM||||quote:<P><B>Also note... The QT primary section also accelerates caliper closing in the secondary circuit. It just won't add fluid volume to the secondary. It's hard to explain.</B><P>I'm intrigued - anything to do with mechanical coupling 'twixt primary and secondary pistons? Please illucidate<P>Thanx and goodnite!||||reg||1
Z||000023||jubjub2m6||11-21-2001||07:38 PM||||I saw that someone didnt think the upgrade made a huge difference as long as you put the new brake master cyl and the porportioning valve on it..mainly just the new brake master cyl that makes such a difference but just make sure you put the stainless steal brake lines on also...i have been very happy...The fiero store has a couple of different sets when i put my new brakes on they were off a 92grand am and i didnt have to grind off anything while a friend of mine got the same set and did have to grind some off..its just pretty much luck of the draw you know....every car is differnt..but if u want some serious stopping power for relatively cheap its a good investment||||reg||1
Z||000024||theogre||11-21-2001||07:43 PM||||Basic rule of fluids.<P>When you take a big volume of fluid and cram it into a smaller space, the fluid accelerates. Just like when a fat river runs into a narrow gorge you get rapids. everything carried in the water is allso accerelated.<P>In this case the fluid acceleration shoves the secondary piston down the bore faster as well as closing it's own calipers faster. You loose some of the effect to drag in the secondary piston and its return spring but it's still faster than a straight bore MC. (The QT blow off valve opens at around 100PSI. That should be more than enough to overcome the secondary return spring.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||artherd||11-21-2001||10:13 PM||||Hey man, no offence meant.   Sounds like you know that a car's brake system, while not an esotic science that only $1bil automakers can enguage in, IS not something to be toyed with recklessly.<P>&lt;images of someone 'slotting' their rotors by hand with a cutting torch, then sanding them down, then putting them on their car...&gt;<P>It'd be intresting to see the (hot) stoping distances of the same car with and withought slotting (or drilling, same overall purpouse.)  With modern low-gas friction material, it shouldn't be much.  But 'low-gas' is not 'no gas' :)<P>If you come up with a successfull way to hub-centralise the rotors, you could do the fiero community a great service by offering the parts to those of us who do not have machine-shop access.<P>I still maintain that the Fiero does need an upgrade to the stock brakes, and that the present "GA" conversions that I have seen, more to the point, their reckless (or lack of) engineering, scare me.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by filthyscarecrow:<BR><B>i was also going to cut an adapter on the lathe to make the rotors hubcentric- to make the round part of the hub fit the round hole in the rotor to keep them in place. nice tight fit. <BR>UK, i wasn't being pissy towards you, it was mostly artherd. i guess there are people on this forum who might not think it a bad idea to test new setups on the busy streets, but i'm not one of them. like i said, we make the brake rotors from scratch for our formula car, and that thing has the most incredible brakes i've ever driven. and yes, they're slotted. <BR>people don't seem to understand slotting rotors. it doesn't cool them down. it's not supposed to. under heavy braking, a thin layer of superhot gas builds up between the pads and the rotors, and the pads effectively "hydroplane" (i don't know the gaseous term) on this layer of gas, and have less contact with the rotors. the slots give that gas somewhere to go adn they also scrape the glaze off the pads, reducing pad fade. i have a set on my integra as well, and the difference is night and day, even over the integra's already kickass brakes. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000026||filthyscarecrow||11-21-2001||11:43 PM||||i was either going to use the mill in teh machine shop at school, or have them CNCd there. <P>torches scare me, hehe... i can just see myself cutting gas slots with a plasma cutter. "hmm, why is there only half a rotor left?"||||reg||1
